 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot H \cdot W)$ for unprojection, where $N$ is number of views, $H, W$ are image dimensions. Filtering complexity depends on implementation (e.g., $O(N_p \log N_p)$ for KDTree-based filtering).
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N \cdot H \cdot W)$ for raw points, reduced after filtering.
-- **Strengths:** Handles overlapping views efficiently; removes redundancy and noise.
-- **Limitations:** Requires accurate calibration; sensitive to depth errors.
-- **When to use:** When constructing a global scene representation from multiple calibrated views.
-- **When not to use:** When real-time single-view reconstruction is required.
+Time and memory complexity are not given in the sources [S1]. The method requires accurate calibration and is sensitive to depth errors [S1]. It is used when constructing a global scene representation from multiple calibrated views [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Originated in works on urban environment modeling, such as [S1] and [S2]. [S1] uses LiDAR for scene bounds and ego-vehicle poses for registration. [S2] focuses on accumulating predicted metric depth maps into a global point cloud for Gaussian Splatting.
+Originated in urban environment modeling and neural scene representations [S1, S2]. [S1] introduces the method for initializing sparse neural volumes in Gaussian Splatting [S1]. [S2] adapts the accumulation pipeline for dynamic urban environments using LiDAR for scene bounds [S2].
 
